title A step that starts no process, and cost nine seconds
prompt The baton and the card: queue item 1, REDS %642's path-refusal fold, left as this ship's own ask last lap.
rota lap 5286, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; read through foundations/20260818-081438_the-three-depths-of-removal.md. Cut and stop. The reading surfaced the stop rather than the cut: a speed lap has its file open, and %804's broken word-split sub_exclude read sits one awk expression away. Molt, breach, debride are depths of removal for writing; the same discipline holds for code. Change the SHAPE, keep the READING. The unquoted printf that preserves the fault is the lap's most deliberate line.
think The elder pathrefuse step spawned no process at all, which is why nobody suspected it. Its cost was is_subex, called once per candidate and walking all 146 sub_exclude entries -- 146 x 9,114, about 1.3 million case evaluations in the shell's own interpreter. So a loop inside a loop is a tell the way a process inside a loop is.
think The exclusion test is the same reading walked the other way. is_subex asks of each ENTRY whether the path equals it or lives under it; a path lives under an entry exactly when one of its own slash-bounded ancestors IS that entry. So the awk walks the path up from itself against a hash of the entries -- the path's depth rather than the roster's length, and the roster may now grow without the step growing with it.
think Equivalence had to be proven rather than argued, since this instrument gates what ships publicly. The step's whole output is three files, so a byte-identical diff of those three over the real 9,114-candidate field settles the step. I then ran the whole projection both ways into two seed directories and compared with diff -r --no-dereference: zero differences over 8,607 copies, 1,428 scrubs, 136 withholds and all three logs. The --no-dereference matters; both trees carry dangling symlinks that diff otherwise follows into errors.
obs A pen proving a fold must plant what the fold could newly get wrong. The elder tested "$x"|"$x"/* whose glob crosses slashes, so DEPTH never mattered and the pen had no deep case; the awk's ancestor walk can stop one level short and still pass every elder leg. Hence room/shut/a/b/deep.md and the mutation that drops the walk -- it ships the deep file while the file-equal entry still refuses, which is exactly the shape a single-level repair would wear.
obs Likewise the ten basename globs became ten anchored regex, and a transcription slip in any one is invisible if the pen proves only four. One file per glob, and a mutation that kills exactly one and must ship exactly one.
obs REDS FIRST FOUND TWO AND THE LEDGER COULD HOLD NEITHER. construction/REDS.md stands at 65,535 bytes against living_pin_max_bytes of 65,536 -- one byte, with three foldable rows on the pin. Both rows are written whole below rather than routed around.
obs ROW ONE (20260917.061322) -- a guard with two ceilings, and the habit watches one. What went wrong: width_check reds the whole fleet at corpus_flagged_files=327 against a ceiling of 326, while corpus_flagged_lines reads 1258 under 1260. The single file over is tools/rye/read_latency.rye, landed 03:43 with four authored usize lines. What caught it: the cold endurance run at this lap's open; the red was not named on the card. What it taught: a ratchet with two ceilings is two ratchets, and a lap that adds usize to an EXISTING flagged file moves only lines, so the habit formed around lines -- a peer log from this same day says so in its own obs. A lap that adds a NEW flagged file moves both, and where the fleet has been shedding lines elsewhere the lines reading stays green and hides the files reading. Beside it: three of the four lines are genuine std seams and the fourth already carries a "// seam:" comment the scan cannot see, because its seam filter recognises a comment LINE and never a seam comment beside a declaration. That is a TAME ruling for read_latency's own lane rather than a sweep, and it is not this seat's file.
obs ROW TWO (20260917.061322) -- the claim law makes commits no nib rule covers. What went wrong: the baton says check, claim, push, THEN build, so a claim commit always lands before the work commit exists; construction/ITINERARY.md's Git nib then names HEAD~2 and remember_git_nib reds for the whole build window. What caught it: the cold endurance run, which recorded remember_git_nib red beside width_check. What it taught: rule 5 of remember-git-nib covers a commit landing AFTER the work commit and has no clause for one landing before it. Grass met the CLOSE half of this at %803 on this same day; this is the OPEN half. incense-nib-writer-two-shapes is mid-build on that very driver and names two shapes, amend and follow-up; the lead-in claim commit is a third. Handed there rather than built beside it.
obs AND A THIRD RED, MINE, CAUGHT WITHIN THE LAP. My account's illustration of the ancestor walk was spelled as a real-looking path, and grass's backtick_path -- built six hours earlier -- read it as a promise: living 65 to 66 against a ceiling of 65. The tree's own law already says it in stamp-and-name: illustrate with placeholders, cite only what exists. Repaired in the card and in the script's comment with segment placeholders; backtick_path and comment_path both GREEN on metal. The red sets of the cold and hot passes differ by exactly this one name, so the change introduced one red and closed it.
obs AND THE BATON'S OWN TWO ORDERS COLLIDE. The cold endurance run wants the tree still; the claim law wants a pushed commit first; tree_digest reads staged, unstaged and untracked alike. So every claiming lap closes tree_moved=yes by obeying both exactly as printed -- which this one did. Third firing of the shape in two days, and a lantern that fires twice is a loom. The cure is one word of ordering on the baton: claim first, then launch the cold run. Both laws hold and nothing else changes.
